     1	# Graded Serre-ideal comparison: proofs and finite certification
     2	
     3	Date: 2026-10-14 (America/Los_Angeles). **The full book conjecture is not proved or refuted here.** This file establishes the exact algebraic meaning of the finite computation and records bounded/special-degree results only. No all-height extrapolation is made.
     4	
     5	## 1. Exact target and conventions
     6	
     7	Work over characteristic zero, first Q and then C. A generalized Cartan matrix A=(a_ij) has a_ii=2, a_ij nonpositive integers for i!=j, and a_ij=0 iff a_ji=0. Use the book's full realization: if rank(A)=ell and A is n by n, take an independent Cartan basis h_1,...,h_(2n-ell) and a rank-n matrix C whose first n rows are A. In the preliminary contragredient algebra g_tilde(A), impose
     8	
     9	    [e_i,f_j]=delta_ij h_i,   [h_p,h_q]=0,
    10	    [h_p,e_i]=c_pi e_i,      [h_p,f_i]=-c_pi f_i.
    11	
    12	The standard triangular decomposition of this preliminary construction is L(f) direct-sum h direct-sum L(e), with free positive and negative Lie algebras. All computations below occur in L=L(e). Its degree is in N^n, with deg(e_i)=epsilon_i and total height |alpha|=sum alpha_i. Full-rank C makes distinct root degrees distinguishable by Cartan weights; in particular h_i are **independent even when A is singular**.
    13	
    14	Let tau be the unique maximal ideal of g_tilde(A) intersecting h trivially. Write K_alpha=tau intersect L_alpha. Let I be the free-Lie ideal of L generated by
    15	
    16	    s_ij=(ad e_i)^(1-a_ij) e_j,  i!=j.
    17	
    18	The full book conjecture says the analogous positive and negative Serre relations generate tau, for every GCM A. Its positive part is I_alpha=K_alpha for every positive multidegree alpha. A discrepancy in one exact-Q multidegree would refute it; agreement through height H proves only that bounded statement. Symmetrizable matrices are already covered by Gabber–Kac. We make no claim of novelty for the basic triangular construction or known symmetrizable theorem.
    19	
    20	## 2. Exact lowering operations
    21	
    22	Use D_i=[f_i, .]. At height one, D_i(e_j)=-delta_ij h_i. At height at least two the result is in the positive free Lie algebra of degree alpha-epsilon_i, or zero. Extend by the derivation identity
    23	
    24	    D_i([u,v])=[D_i(u),v]+[u,D_i(v)].
    25	
    26	When a leaf produces -h_i, use
    27	
    28	    [-h_i,v]=-(sum_j a_ij deg_j(v))v,
    29	    [u,-h_i]=+(sum_j a_ij deg_j(u))u.
    30	
    31	The row index is i: interchanging a_ij and a_ji would change the computation for a nonsymmetrizable matrix. The producer implements this recursive rule.
    32	
    33	There is an independent tensor-word formula used by the checker. Expand a positive Lie element x of height at least two in the free associative algebra as x=sum_w b_w w. The enveloping-algebra commutator [f_i,w] inserts -h_i at each occurrence of e_i. Commuting h_i to the right gives
    34	
    35	    D_i(x) = -sum_w b_w sum_(p:w_p=i)
    36	                 (sum_(q>p) a_(i,w_q)) (w with position p deleted).
    37	
    38	The rightmost-h_i coefficient is -sum_w b_w sum_(p:w_p=i) (w with p deleted). It vanishes on every Lie element of height at least two: it is the associative derivation with e_i mapped to 1 and the other generators mapped to 0, which annihilates every nontrivial Lie bracket. The checker verifies this cancellation explicitly. Thus the word-deletion formula is not an approximation to the recursive lowering rule; it is a separately derived exact formula.
    39	
    40	## 3. Proposition: the radical recurrence computes the maximal ideal
    41	
    42	Set K_epsilon_i=0. For |alpha|>=2 define recursively
    43	
    44	    K_alpha={x in L_alpha : D_i(x) belongs to K_(alpha-epsilon_i) for every i}.
    45	
    46	An absent or negative multidegree means the zero space. Then these spaces equal tau intersect L_alpha in every positive degree.
    47	
    48	**Proof.** First let K+=direct-sum_alpha K_alpha. By definition it is stable under each f_i; at height one it has no vectors, so lowering never produces a nonzero Cartan component. It is stable under h because each homogeneous component is a weight space.
    49	
    50	To show stability under e_j, use induction on the height of x in K_alpha. For every i,
    51	
    52	    D_i([e_j,x])=[e_j,D_i(x)]-delta_ij alpha(h_i)x.
    53	
    54	The first term lies in K_(alpha+epsilon_j-epsilon_i) by induction applied to the lower-height element D_i(x); the second lies there as well when i=j. Hence [e_j,x] satisfies the defining recurrence for K_(alpha+epsilon_j). The base case has K_epsilon_i=0. Thus K+ is an ideal of g_tilde(A), contained entirely in the positive part. It meets h trivially, so K+ is contained in tau.
    55	
    56	Conversely, tau intersect L_epsilon_i=0, because otherwise its bracket with f_i contains the nonzero h_i. For x in tau intersect L_alpha of greater height, each D_i(x) belongs to tau in a lower positive degree. Induction and the recurrence imply x in K_alpha. This proves equality. QED.
    57	
    58	This proof uses the ordinary characteristic-zero contragredient construction with full Cartan realization. It does not identify a positive-characteristic group kernel with a Lie ideal. Finite-field computations in this folder are separate rank diagnostics.
    59	
    60	## 4. Proposition: the recursive Serre span is exactly I
    61	
    62	Let S_alpha be the span of original Serre generators of degree alpha. Then
    63	
    64	    I_alpha = S_alpha + sum_i [e_i, I_(alpha-epsilon_i)].
    65	
    66	**Proof.** The right-hand recursive family contains the prescribed generators and is stable under bracket with every e_i. Repeated Jacobi identities imply stability under bracket with every element of the Lie algebra generated by the e_i, namely L. It is therefore an ideal containing all s_ij, and contains I by minimality. The reverse containment follows by induction, since every displayed generator/bracket belongs to I. QED.
    67	
    68	Moreover I is contained in K+. For r>=1, direct induction from Section 2 gives
    69	
    70	    D_i((ad e_i)^r e_j)
    71	      = -r(a_ij+r-1)(ad e_i)^(r-1)e_j.
    72	
    73	At r=1-a_ij this is zero. Lowering by f_j gives (ad e_i)^r(-h_j): for r>=2 it is zero after the second commutator; for r=1 we have a_ij=0 and hence a_ji=0, so it is also zero. Lowering by any other f_k is zero. Thus each Serre generator is killed by every lowering operator and belongs to K at its height. Since K+ is an ideal, I is contained in K+.
    74	
    75	## 5. Finite algorithm and independent certificate
    76	
    77	For each multidegree through a fixed height H, enumerate the standard Lyndon words over the ordered alphabet e_1<e_2<e_3. Bracket a Lyndon word w by splitting off its longest proper Lyndon suffix v and recursively taking [b(u),b(v)]. In its associative expansion the lexicographically least word is w with coefficient 1. This triangularity allows exact conversion from Lie polynomials to Lyndon coordinates.
    78	
    79	The number of such words in multidegree alpha is the multigraded Witt dimension
    80	
    81	    dim L_alpha = (1/|alpha|) sum_(d | gcd(alpha)) mu(d)
    82	                  (|alpha|/d)! / product_i (alpha_i/d)!.
    83	
    84	This identity also follows from the PBW product formula for the enveloping algebra of a free Lie algebra, whose tensor-algebra Hilbert series is 1/(1-sum t_i), by taking logarithms and applying Möbius inversion. The monic triangular expansions give linear independence; the dimension identity gives completeness. These standard free-Lie facts justify the basis, and the checker verifies the dimension identity and triangular expansions in every computed degree.
    85	
    86	The producer constructs I_alpha from Section 4. It constructs the map
    87	
    88	    L_alpha -> direct-sum_i L_(alpha-epsilon_i)/K_(alpha-epsilon_i),
    89	    x |-> (D_i x)_i,
    90	
    91	and obtains K_alpha as its exact kernel by Section 3. It records spanning rows for both ideals, free basis words, and dimensions. Height-one kernels are explicitly zero; they are not inferred by wrongly treating Cartan elements as zero.
    92	
    93	The independent script `work/check_certificate.py` imports neither the producer nor its library. It works in associative-word coordinates and uses the word-deletion lowering formula of Section 2. For every degree it checks:
    94	
    95	1. all and only the expected Lyndon words occur; their count is the Witt dimension;
    96	2. certificate ideal rows are independent and span exactly the original Serre generators and brackets with preceding certificate ideal rows;
    97	3. certificate radical rows are independent, have zero projected descents modulo preceding radical rows, and have exactly the nullity obtained from an independent exact rational rank computation;
    98	4. I_alpha is contained in K_alpha and all reported dimensions match these ranks;
    99	5. no degree with nonzero free-Lie dimension is omitted or duplicated.
   100	
   101	These conditions certify equality with the recursively defined spaces, not merely a numerical agreement between two totals. Negative controls alter the characteristic, omit a degree, and change a reported dimension. The checker must reject all of them. The source hashes, certificate hash, and actual run results are recorded separately in evidence/.
   102	
   103	Every coefficient in these finite matrices is rational. Tensoring finite-dimensional spaces and exact sequences with C is exact; therefore verified Q dimensions agree with those for the book's characteristic-zero C realization. A modular rank match by itself would not suffice: ranks may jump at primes, and a modular kernel is not a counterexample over C.
   104	
   105	## 6. Fixed matrix families and scope
   106	
   107	The input matrices were written to `work/matrices.json` before the computations. Two controls are finite type A3 and affine type A2. Three nonsymmetrizable matrices are:
   108	
   109	    A_one = [[2,-1,-1],[-1,2,-1],[-2,-1,2]],
   110	    A_cycle = [[2,-1,-2],[-2,2,-1],[-1,-2,2]],
   111	    A_large = [[2,-2,-2],[-3,2,-2],[-2,-3,2]].
   112	
   113	For a fully connected rank-three GCM, existence of a nonzero diagonal symmetrizer requires
   114	
   115	    a_12 a_23 a_31 = a_21 a_32 a_13.
   116	
   117	Indeed, multiply the three equations d_i a_ij=d_j a_ji around the cycle and cancel d_1 d_2 d_3. Conversely, this condition permits construction of consistent diagonal entries by ratios along two edges. The respective absolute cycle products for the three matrices are 2 versus 1, 1 versus 8, and 8 versus 18. Hence all three are genuinely nonsymmetrizable, not rank-two or tree-shaped instances already covered by symmetrization.
   118	
   119	The finite runs target **all** positive multidegrees of height at most 10, not just diagonal degrees. A missing free-Lie degree has zero dimension and is treated as zero throughout. The exact run and independent-check receipts determine the exercised bound; this file does not claim any larger unexercised range.
   120	
   121	## 7. An all-degree coefficient-one boundary theorem
   122	
   123	**Theorem.** Let J be a set of indices and i an additional index. Suppose the principal GCM A_J is symmetrizable. For every positive degree alpha supported on J union {i} with alpha_i=1, the Serre ideal and maximal Cartan-disjoint ideal agree: I_alpha=K_alpha.
   124	
   125	This is a consequence of established symmetrizable theory, not a claim to solve the nonsymmetrizable conjecture. It is proved uniformly in all remaining coefficients of alpha and all allowed entries of A.
   126	
   127	**Proof by symmetrizable comparison.** We may restrict to the supported principal submatrix: free positive degrees, Serre spans, and lowerings with indices in the support are unchanged, while outside lowerings are zero.
   128	
   129	Choose positive integers d_j symmetrizing A_J. Such an integer choice exists after clearing denominators of a positive rational symmetrizer. On J union {i}, construct a GCM B by retaining every row indexed by J and putting
   130	
   131	    b_ij=d_j a_ji for j in J,   b_ii=2.
   132	
   133	Then diag((d_j)_(j in J),1) symmetrizes B. Its entries are integers with the required signs and symmetric zero pattern.
   134	
   135	Let S(A) and S(B) denote the Serre quotients, not the maximal-ideal quotients. Their positive spaces with coefficient of i equal to one are canonically identical. Indeed, all Serre generators whose leading index lies in J are unchanged. If a_ij<0, a Serre generator with leading index i contains at least two occurrences of e_i, so it and all positive descendants cannot contribute to an i-coefficient-one component. If a_ij=0, then a_ji=b_ij=0 and the commutation relation [e_i,e_j]=0 is unchanged. This proves equality of the defining positive ideal components in the entire slice, not just in a truncated degree.
   136	
   137	Under this identification, each lowering D_j with j in J is unchanged, because its formula depends only on row j of the matrix. Let W be the image in this slice of the extra ideal tau(A)/I(A). It is graded, stable under all D_j with j in J, and its component at epsilon_i is zero.
   138	
   139	By the Gabber–Kac theorem for the symmetrizable B, S(B)=g(B). Its invariant symmetric bilinear form gives a nondegenerate pairing between each nonzero root space g(B)_alpha and its opposite g(B)_(-alpha). For x in W_alpha, every element of the opposite root space is a linear combination of iterated brackets
   140	
   141	    ad(f_j1) ... ad(f_jm) f_i,   all j_k in J,
   142	
   143	with exactly alpha_j occurrences of each index j in J. This spanning statement follows from Jacobi: a negative bracket word containing exactly one f_i can be rewritten with that f_i innermost and all other factors acting on it.
   144	
   145	Move these adjoint operators across the invariant pairing. Up to a sign, the result is a pairing of f_i with a composition of J-lowerings applied to x. All intermediate vectors stay in W, and the final degree is epsilon_i, whose W component is zero. Hence x pairs to zero with the whole opposite root space. Nondegeneracy gives x=0. Thus W=0 in the slice, proving I_alpha=K_alpha. QED.
   146	
   147	**Rank-three corollary.** Every rank-two GCM is symmetrizable: for [[2,-p],[-r,2]] with p,r>0, diag(r,p) is a symmetrizer; the disconnected case is immediate. Therefore for every rank-three GCM, equality holds at every degree having a zero or one coordinate. Any additional positive relation must have all three coordinates at least two. In particular the entire height-at-most-five range is excluded uniformly, and (2,2,2) at height six is the first possible full-support degree. This is much stronger than a finite sample but still leaves infinitely many interior degrees.
   148	
   149	The proof does not assume that a singular rank-two Cartan matrix has independent rows. If using the alternative lowest-weight-module proof, a full Levi Cartan realization must be chosen inside the ambient full realization. The comparison proof above avoids that extra representation-theoretic bookkeeping.
   150	
   151	## 8. Necessary condition on a first extra relation
   152	
   153	Suppose the extra ideal tau/I is nonzero, and choose a nonzero homogeneous element x of least positive height. Then every D_i x is zero, by minimality. In the Serre quotient the adjoint actions of e_i and f_i are locally nilpotent: this holds on each Chevalley/Cartan generator by the Serre relations and the sl2 relations, and hence on every finite bracket by the iterated derivation rule. The sl2-submodule generated by the lowest-weight vector x is therefore finite-dimensional. Its lowest h_i-weight is a nonpositive integer. Consequently
   154	
   155	    sum_j a_ij alpha_j <= 0 for every i.
   156	
   157	For a rank-three counterexample, combine this with Section 7: a first extra relation can only occur at an interior degree alpha_j>=2 in the fundamental imaginary cone A alpha<=0. This is a necessary condition, not an existence result. The computed examples include such interior degrees rather than testing only the already-excluded boundary.
   158	
   159	## 9. Remaining mathematical gap
   160	
   161	Agreement at finite height does not imply I=K in all heights. The recurrence constructs an exact search for a first additional relation, but supplies no uniform bound on where a first relation would occur if one exists. A proof of the book conjecture requires an all-height argument for every nonsymmetrizable GCM, not three matrices and not a finite-dimensional truncation. Conversely a modular discrepancy needs exact characteristic-zero confirmation and explicit witness verification before it can be called a counterexample.

