    83	         The notion of a root groupoid presented in this paper is close to the one defined in [19].
    84	
    85	      1.1.2. The connection between semisimple Lie algebras and root systems can be described
    86	      from two opposite perspectives. One can start with a Lie algebra, choose a Cartan sub-
    87	      algebra and study the geometry of the set of roots. On the other hand, one can start
    88	      with a Cartan matrix and construct a Lie algebra by generators and relations. The second
    89	      approach was vastly extended to construct a very important family of infinite-dimensional
    90	      Lie algebras by Kac, Moody, Borcherds and others. Our approach follows the same pattern
    91	      for the construction of Lie superalgebras from combinatorial data.
    92	
    93	      1.1.3. The standard construction of a Kac–Moody Lie algebra comprises two steps. In
    94	      the first step one defines a huge graded Lie algebra factoring a free Lie algebra by so-called
    95	      Chevalley relations (we call this algebra the half-baked algebra). In the second step one
    96	      factors the half-baked algebra by the unique maximal ideal having trivial intersection with
    97	      the Cartan subalgebra (this is Victor Kac’s approach). Alternatively (Robert Moody) one
    98	      imposes an explicit set of Serre relations. Both approaches yield the same result in the
    99	      symmetrizable case.
   100	         One of the motivations of our work came from the observation that the classical Serre
   101	      relations can be interpreted as reflected Chevalley relations. This led us to the notion
   102	      of a root algebra which, roughly speaking, respects the symmetries determined by a root
   103	      groupoid. In many cases there is a unique root algebra which can be defined by Chevalley
   104	      relations reflected in all possible ways. Sometimes there is a number of root algebras
   105	      defined by a given root datum. The description of all root algebras is an open question
   106	      — we don’t know the answer even for Lie algebras. For finite dimensional and affine Lie
   107	      superalgebras all Serre relations were described in [23], see also [24]. One can see from
   108	      this description that Serre relations may involve more than two generators.
   109	
   110	      1.2. Root groupoid. In [11], 1.1, Kac defines a realization of a Cartan matrix A = (axy ),
   111	      x, y ∈ X, as a triple (h, a, b) such that a = {a(x) ∈ h}, b = {b(x) ∈ h∗ } and ⟨a(x), b(x)⟩ =
   112	      axy . Adapting this definition to Lie superalgebras, we add the parity function p : X → Z2
   113	      on the index set X and make a quadruple v = (h, a, b, p) an object of the root groupoid
   114	      R — the main object of our study. Every quadruple v defines a Cartan matrix by the
   115	      formula above. The pair (A, p) is called Cartan datum. There are three kinds of generators
   116	      in the set of arrows in R. Two of them are quite dull: one (a homothety) rescales a(x),
   117	      another (tautological arrow) is defined by an isomorphism θ : h → h′ ; the third kind are
   118	      reflexions that retain the same vector space h but change the collections a(x) and b(x)
   119	      by usual (even or odd) reflection formulas. Each generator f : v → v ′ defines a linear
   120	      transformation hv → hv′ (it is the identity for homotheties and reflexions, and θ for the
   121	      tautological arrow defined by θ); two compositions of generators leading from v to v ′ are
   122	      equivalent if they define the same linear transformation. The root groupoid R has a lot of
   123	      components, some of them, most probably, useless. However, some connected components
   124	      (we call them admissible) lead to interesting Lie superalgebras. It is worth mentioning
   125	      that Cartan matrices Av are different even inside one component: one type of reflexions,
   126	      isotropic reflexions, modify Cartan matrices in a certain way (see the formulas in § 2.2.2).
   127	
